-- CC_0287_trivia_options_scale_years.sql
--
-- Give 234 year-dated trivia questions a 7-mark `options_scale`, the second half of the
-- numeric backfill. CC_0286 did the real quantities; years were held back because they
-- need a test the quantities did not.
--
-- 🔴 "A YEAR QUESTION MUST NOT OFFER A FUTURE YEAR" IS WRONG, AND MEASURABLY SO.
-- Thirteen active questions correctly offer years after today -- "In what year is the
-- next presidential election scheduled?" among them. The question is never whether a
-- mark lies in the future; it is whether a mark lies on the WRONG SIDE OF TODAY for the
-- event being asked about.
--
-- The answer settles that without parsing tense, which would have been the guess. An
-- answer at or before this year is an event that HAS happened, so no mark may be later
-- than this year: nothing happened in 2031. An answer after this year is an event still
-- to come, so no mark may be earlier than this year: the NEXT election was not in 2024.
-- Verified on the output: 0 of 234 proposals cross their own side of today.
--
-- CADENCE IS KEPT, NOT SHRUNK. Where the four stored options are evenly spaced, that
-- spacing is a real claim -- elections run every two or four years, policy targets land
-- on 2030, 2035, 2040. Shrinking it to make room produces marks like 2026/2029/2032
-- around an answer of 2035, where the answer is the only round year offered, and that is
-- a tell. So an evenly spaced year set keeps its cadence or is refused; 6 were refused
-- on exactly this ground.
--
-- WHY 168 WERE REFUSED. A refusal is the correct outcome: `options_scale` is nullable and
-- servePresentation.ts falls back to the stored `options`, so a refused question behaves
-- exactly as it does today. The run considered every numeric question still unscaled,
-- which is why the bounded series and ordinals from CC_0286's refusal list appear again.
--
-- IDEMPOTENT, and guarded on the same invariant the serve path checks: the UPDATE skips
-- any row that already has a scale, and any row whose stored answer no longer equals the
-- scale's centre mark.

BEGIN;

